Herein, we present a novel synergistic strategy combining etching and protecting process for preparing shelled Co-based zeolitic imidazolate (ZIF-67) rhombic dodecahedral cages (RDCs) with an inner hollow cavity, then transform them into hollow \(\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{N}\) -doped carbon nanocages \(\mathrm{Co}@\) NCNs) via a pyrolysis process, which address the shortcomings using sacrificing templates or corrosive agents. The compactly aggregated Co particles can be avoided effectively by washing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution, favoring the formation of a denser magnetic coupling network. Owing to the large specific surface area, hierarchical conductive micro-meso-macroporous network, highly dispersive Co particles distribution, large core-shell \(\mathrm{Co}@\) graphitic carbon heterojunctions, as well as matched impedance characteristic, an optimal reflection loss of \(-60.6\) dB and wide absorption bandwidth of \(5.1\ \mathrm{GHz}\) , can be achieved with \(10\,\mathrm{\wt}\%\) filler loading, exhibiting superior specific reflection loss compared with the vast majority of previous MOFs derived counterparts.

# 2. Results and Discussion

Figure 1 illustrates the synthetic procedure of hollow \(\mathrm{Co}@\) NCNs. First, ZIF-67 polyhedrons were prepared by a modified method,[22] then shelled ZIF-67 RDCs with inner hollow cavity were obtained through a synergistic process by using tannic acid (TA) as the protecting-etching agent. Concretely, the relatively large TA molecules with planar conformation and abundant hydroxyl groups attached on ZIF-67 surface because of their strong coating ability and preserved the exposed surface, free protons released from the TA solution (the initial pH value is 3.83 at the same concentration) penetrated into the inner region of ZIF-67 through the interconnected pores/channels (the diameters of the intrinsic open channels are smaller than \(2\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) and were confined in this region, which were used to destroy the weak \(\mathrm{Co-N}\) bonding, thus the etching process occurred in the central region.[23] The hollow structure of shelled ZIF-67 RDCs is mainly determined by the etching time as shown in Figure S1, Supporting Information. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) observation shows that when ZIF-67 polyhedrons are subjected to etching for 2 min in TA solution, a similar rhombic dodecahedral structure is inherited compared with the initial ZIF-67 polyhedrons, the shadows (indicated by the red arrows) reveal the center of ZIF-67 is partly removed. After a reaction of \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) , macrosized inner void and outer shell with a thickness of \(65{-}90\ \mathrm{nm}\) are observed, suggesting the

successful formation of shelled ZIF-67 RDCs with inner hollow cavity. To clarify the attached TA molecules protect the exposed ZIF-67 surface from etching, the surface zeta-potentials of ZIF-67 and shelled ZIF-67 RDCs are compared. The zetapotential of ZIF-67 varies from positive \((+16\,\mathrm{\mV})\) to negative \((-33\ \mathrm{\mV})\) after the protecting-etching process as illustrated in Figure S2, Supporting Information, the significant change clearly indicates that TA is adsorbed on the surface, preserving the outer crystalline framework. The adsorption of TA is further supported by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) in Figure S3, Supporting Information, the vibration and stretching bands are assignable to the adsorbed TA molecules.[24] X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern in Figure S4, Supporting Information, confirms that shelled ZIF-67 RDCs possess the same crystal structure as that of ZIF-67 polyhedrons, indicating the etching process can not affect the crystalline phase and no new product generates. After a subsequent pyrolysis process, shelled ZIF-67 RDCs are converted into hollow \(\mathrm{Co@N}\) CNs and the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) species are in situ reduced to Co particles at high temperature under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere. Without washing by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution, TEM images in Figure S5, Supporting Information, prove that reduced Co particles tend to aggregate, failing to utilize the magnetic coupling to generate a strong magnetic loss. In Figure S6, Supporting Information, the results illustrate that washing by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution, uniform Co particles with a diameter of \(10{-}20\ \mathrm{nm}\) are encapsulated by granitic carbon, forming core-shell microstructures and uniform heterojunctions. The discrete distribution of Co particles with smaller diameters may radiate network-like magnetic flux lines in carbon matrix and trigger strong magnetic coupling according to the previous report.[25]

The morphology and size of the obtained hollow \(\mathrm{Co@N}\) CNs are displayed in Figure 2. As elucidated from the observation of a broken particle by the red arrows, the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images clearly reveal that \(\mathrm{Co@N}\) CNs, inheriting the rhombic dodecahedron of ZIF-67, possesses a hollow structure compared with the SEM images of solid \(\mathrm{Co@NC-800}\) (Figure S7, Supporting Information). TEM images also unambiguously demonstrate the large internal cavity for hollow \(\mathrm{Co}@\) NCNs products. For hollow \(\mathrm{Co@N}\) CNs-600, we can observe that these reduced Co particles dispersedly distribute in carbon matrix with an average size of about \(5\ \mathrm{nm}\) , the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image shows that the lattice spacing of \(0.205\ \mathrm{nm}\) corresponds to the (111) lattice plane of Co particles and the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) image (inset in Figure 2d) confirms the amorphous structure of carbon matrix. By increasing the pyrolysis temperature to \(800\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , the rhombic dodecahedron with hollow structure and homogeneous Co particles distribution can be well retained. Simultaneously, the HRTEM image reveals that Co particles are encapsulated by graphitic carbon layers with an interplanar spacing of \(0.304\ \mathrm{nm}\) , confirming the formation of uniform core-shell heterojunctions between magnetic Co particles and dielectric graphitic carbon. As revealed in Figure S8, Supporting Information, high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) and element distribution maps indicate that C, N, and Co elements are uniformly distributed in hollow \(\mathrm{Co@N}\) CNs-800. As illustrated in Figure 2i–l, it is clear that the rhombic dodecahedron collapses to some degree for hollow
