The crystal structure of the samples is investigated by XRD pattern in Figure 3a. Three diffraction peaks at \(44.2^{\circ}\), \(51.5^{\circ}\), and \(75.9^{\circ}\) are indexed to the (111), (200), and (220) planes of alpha Co (PDF#89-4307), suggesting the existence of Co particles. Interestingly, the inconspicuous diffraction peak at about \(26^{\circ}\) for Co@NCNs-600 demonstrates the amorphous carbon, corresponding to the result of SAED image (inset in Figure 2c). However, the characteristic diffraction peaks of graphitic carbon and Co particles become obvious for hollow Co@NCNs-800 and hollow Co@NCNs-1000, implying the increase of pyrolysis temperature promotes the graphitization degree of carbon and leads to the aggregation of Co particles. During the pyrolysis process, the possible reactions, such as the generation of gases, the mobility of heteroatoms, and the reconstruction of graphitic carbon skeleton, determine the intensity ratio \((I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})\) of graphitic carbon in Raman spectra (Figure 3b). It is clear that, for hollow Co@NCNs, the \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) value decreases from 1.01 to 0.92 as the pyrolysis temperature increases, suggesting an enhanced graphitization degree of carbon skeleton. \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherms are measured to collect the specific surface area and pore size distribution. As shown in Figure 3c, the sharply increased trend at low pressure \((\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}<0.1)\) implies the presence of micropores while the hysteresis loop at high pressure region suggests the significant amount of meso-macropores for hollow Co@NCNs. Concretely, the specific surface area of hollow Co@NCNs-800 reaches \(571.08\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and the pore volume is as high as \(1.5545\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) examined by the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller

