In order to investigate the associated microwave absorption mechanism, the electromagnetic parameters of all samples with \(10\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) filler loading are presented in Figure 4. In the frequency range of \(2{-}18\ \mathrm{GHz}\), hollow Co@NCNs-600 displays the lowest complex permittivity \(\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}\right.\) and \(\left.\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)\) due to the formation of amorphous carbon frameworks with inferior electrical conductivity at a relatively low pyrolysis temperature. With increasing the pyrolysis temperature, the higher crystallinity of Co particles promotes the formation of graphitization degree, resulting in higher electrical conductivity and increased complex permittivity based on the free-electron theory. From the viewpoint of structural design and the theoretical guidance, hollow engineering will establish a more conductive network compared with solid counterparts, thus hollow Co@NCNs-800 and Co@NCNs-1000 gain higher \(\varepsilon^{\prime}\) and \(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\) values than
