NCNs-800 reach as high as 606 and 252.5, surpassing the vast majority of reported MOFs derived counterparts, (Figure S13, Supporting Information), which can be served as promising candidates for lightweight and highly efficient microwave absorbers.

In general, the microwave energy loss mechanism of hollow Co@NCNs is mainly dominated by impedance matching, conductive loss as well as the synergistic effect between polarization loss and magnetic loss. First, in our synthesized carbon-based composites, the hierarchical micro-meso-macropores are beneficial for decreasing the impedance gap between the absorbers and air, leading to promoted impedance matching. Hollow engineering induces multiple microwave scattering and causes the propagation path inside the void to get further attenuation. Second, as for conductive loss, Co particles with diameter in the range of nanometer are almost insulating owing to the small size effect,[26] thus the electrical conductivity is mainly dominated by carbon components with high graphitization degree, which is much more sensitive to the pyrolysis temperature. Obviously, the graphitization degree of carbon skeleton builds a conductive network and favors the transmission of electrons, leading to enhanced conductive loss.[27] For solid structure, there is an obvious space barrier between each absorber in the paraffin matrix, thus the electron hopping among them has been inhibited, as shown in Figure 5a. The construction of a hollow structure can significantly increase the volumetric fraction, in which a more conductive network can be established
